Z||000000||Eric||04-23-2001||08:46 PM||||I bought a new master cylinder and I'm having trouble bleeding it.  There seems to be a fountain of brake fluid coming from the forward brakes fluid resevoir.  There is no fluid escaping from the hole to the brake lines while the piston is being depressed; it's all coming out the top.  Is there something wrong with the cylinder, or am I not bleeding it right?  I did use a vacuum pump to bleed it too.||||reg||8
Z||000001||theogre||04-23-2001||09:33 PM||||sounds like the new MC is bad||||reg||1
Z||000002||Eric||04-23-2001||09:38 PM||||Everyone else thinks so too except for the guy who sold it to me.  It just seems odd that the fluid is coming out of the wrong hole, almost like it was put together wrong.  The Ogre says "take it back" and I think I'll do just that.  Has anyone else had this experience?||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||04-23-2001||09:45 PM||||Ogre, is it common for fluid to "fountain" from the resovior when the brake pedal is depressed?  My questionable m/c does the same but from the rear circuit side, but fluid also escapes from the circuit hole.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-23-2001||09:57 PM||||You will get a tiny backwash from the front circuit as the secondary piston moves forward. This is normal. It take a mm or 2 of piston travel to close the port to the reserve tank.<P>You will get considerable backwash from the primary(rear) circuit because of the 2 stage bore. In that case the backwash is mainly excess fluid from the rear bore.<P>(There's a diagram in my brake upgrade article that explains how the "Step Bore" MC works.)<P>In the above case it sounds allot like the rear seal on the secondary piston is in backward. (That or damaged.) This would allow fluid from the primary circuit to flow around the secondary piston ant flow out the compensation holes.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric||04-23-2001||10:01 PM||||Okay, which is front and which is rear.  The hole that it fountains from is the one closest to the booster or back of the car.  56, did you save your old master cylinder to see if it did the same thing?  When you bench bled it did fluid squirt out both holes equally?  Mine barely squirts any fluid out that hole at all while it shoots out of the other one.  If I remember correctly you've been fighting a mushy pedal for some time now.  Maybe you have the same problem I do to a lesser degree.  I also tried to vacuum bleed the whole line caliper and master cylinder on the car while hooked up.  I got fluid out of the rear calipers, but nothing out of the fronts.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eric||04-23-2001||10:26 PM||||I read the brake article on your site, Ogre.  The fountaining is coming from the hole in the primary reserve.  I'm not sure if this changes your hypothesis about the piston being in backwards or not.  Could you please clarify?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eric||04-23-2001||10:27 PM||||I should say the seal could be in backwards not the piston.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Eric (edited 04-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||04-23-2001||10:32 PM||||Eric, I checked this on my '86, and it does the same thing, but not as bad as my questionable m/c. The rear section of resoviour(nearest the booster) on my questionable m/c shoots a fountain of fluid during the entire pedal stroke. The front only shoots for a few mm of travel like Ogre said, then no more out the front.<P>Mine shoots fluid out what appears to be equally out both holes(where brake lines plug in). I have been fighting a mushy pedal for some time, no sucess yet <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">. I turned in my original m/c as the core.<P>If you're getting nothing out the front circuit, I'd got with Ogres thinking(he knows WAY more than I ever will) about the bad/backwards secondary seal(front smaller seal).||||reg||1
Z||000009||Eric||04-23-2001||10:42 PM||||I don't want to rebuild it.  If it's bad, I'll just send it back.  I guess I'll try to bench bleed it again to make sure there's no fluid coming out of the front circuit before it goes back.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||04-24-2001||08:04 PM||||Eric,<P>Well.... <P>If you take 2 paper cups and hold them with the bottoms together then you have the secondary piston. (They don't call'em cup seals for nothin'  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> )<P>IF the rear seal on the secondary(front) piston is backward fluid will flow from the primary(rear) chamber out the secondary compensation hole instead of pushing the piston like it should. (The 2 pistons are actually fluid coupled.)<P>F56, In you case it sounds like the flow valve is bad. The best way to understand that valve it to look at the diagrams in the patents listed in the brake upgrade article. The valve can fail at least 3 ways. If you'res is squirting up I'd suspect...<P>the spring is weak/broken<BR>the blowoff valve is blocked open with dirt.<P>either way that MC is probably toast as well.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Eric||04-24-2001||09:52 PM||||I think 56 has a Formula and I have an 88GT.  I think the master cylinder design is different for this year and might have different characteristics.  That doesn't change the fact that it doesn't work right.  I'm picking up a new one tommorow.  If the same thing happens, I'll be inclined to think it's my fault.  Is there any bleeding tips, recomendations that anyone could give me other than what's in the manual or what's on Ogre's site?||||reg||1
